cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyunsam (동현삼): a single-player Three Kingdoms strategy simulation game on a map of 41 provinces with hex battles, duels and fire attacks, with starting values and rule numbers set through the esoteric language Donghyun-lang"
type: software
version: "1.11"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24106052"
date-released: "2026-11-19"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Raspberry-Kim/donghyun-lab16"
url: "https://raspberry-kim.github.io/donghyun-lab16/"
abstract: >-
  Version 1.11 redraws the map as soon as an example is chosen, so the
  starting year and the division of land of that example are seen before
  the game is opened; a game in progress is replaced only after a
  confirmation, and the start card moved to a small box in a corner of
  the map. Troops to move or to send into an attack, and gold and rice to
  transport, are now set as numbers (steps of 100 and 1,000, typed digits,
  or the shortcuts none, half and all) instead of choosing half or all.
  Rules, data, the save format and the AI are unchanged.
  Version 1.10 added six scenarios besides the base setup of January 200
  (December 189, November 194, October 201, September 208, April 221 and
  February 235; 4 to 15 rulers each, 97 more historical figures), chosen
  with the new setting 31; only the starting year and month follow the
  scenarios of Sangokushi DS 2, while the division of land, the placement
  of officers and every number are new to this work. Setting 9 multiplies
  the gold, rice and popular support of the player's provinces only, and
  seven "easy" examples use it at 300 percent. Several officers can now
  be moved in one order. Unaffiliated officers in the player's provinces
  are announced by the second companion at each new month and shown on
  the map, on the search button and in the province details, so a search
  is not wasted on an empty province. The province details are laid out
  as a compact grid. Offices are described as a ruler's action outside
  the one-order-per-province-per-month rule. Settings grew from 32 to 34,
  examples from 13 to 26 (in five groups) and rule notes from 120 to 124.
  Saves up to version 1.00 load into the base setup.
  Version 1.00 is the first full release. It adds marching animation
  along the roads between castles (moves, supply carts and invasions),
  three offices (a governor per province, one advisor and a few generals
  per realm) with delegation of a province to its governor, three new
  domestic values (flood control, commerce and troop drill) with seasonal
  events (plague, flood, locusts, bumper harvest) and season-tinted land,
  one-on-one duels between officers on the battlefield (four moves with a
  multiplier table; the loser is captured), and fire attacks with wind,
  rain and spreading flames on a new forest terrain. The player is
  addressed as "my lord" at first, "Your Highness" after becoming king at
  10 provinces and "Your Majesty" after becoming emperor at 14; the rank
  never drops. Donghyun-lang settings grew from 22 to 32 and the examples
  from 11 to 13; the second companion now has 120 rule notes. The choice
  of these elements was informed by which features exist in Sangokushi
  DS 2 (Koei, 2007), a Nintendo DS remake of Romance of the Three
  Kingdoms IV; every formula and number is new to this work, and no
  graphic, sound, text, number or code of that game is used. The save
  format number is unchanged and saves up to version 0.36 still load.
  Version 0.36 softened the border of the desktop-recommended notice box
  to a pastel coral and let it blink only ten times after the page opens,
  then stay lit.
  Version 0.35 made that border blink in red, once a second.
  Version 0.34 added a victory celebration: on winning, a synthesized
  fanfare of about ten seconds plays the first line of the trio tune of
  Elgar's Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1 (public domain; no recording
  is used) while pixel flower petals fall over the map, leaving the
  result card clear. Defeat keeps its earlier sound.
  Version 0.33 reworded the opening line that the second companion speaks on
  the first screen, before a game is started. Her portrait is the Nagasaki
  port associate of Donghyunhang (the first of the two Nagasaki faces) and
  the first companion keeps the Jemulpo associate, as in version 0.32.
  Since version 0.30 two companions sit at the top of the control panel,
  shown from the first screen: one recites one of 100 passages from the Zhuangzi
  on the player's own provinces and, on other provinces, briefly describes
  the place and reports its ruler and troop numbers computed from the live
  game state; the other gives one of 75 short notes (120 since version 1.00, 124 since 1.10)
  on the rules and their arithmetic, drawn from the current rule values
  without repeats. The map
  opens at 1.125x in both normal and full-screen view.
  Donghyunsam is a browser-based, noncommercial toy that follows only the
  broad frame of Romance of the Three Kingdoms II (Koei, 1989): the player
  picks one of nine rulers on a node map of 41 provinces of late Han China
  and gives one order per province per month (domestic work, draft, drill,
  move, invade, search, alliance; domestic work is farmland, flood control,
  commerce or relief). A province with no officer becomes
  vacant land that an officer can occupy by moving in. An invasion opens a 10x10 hex battlefield with terrain
  movement costs and defence modifiers; the other rulers are played by a
  simple priority-based AI. Starting values, rule numbers, the victory
  condition and time limit are set through a program written in the esoteric
  programming language Donghyun-lang, whose integer output is read as pairs
  of setting number and value by a shared adapter. The game runs on a small
  common engine (fixed 60-tick loop, calendar, square and hex grids with A*
  path finding, tile map renderer, scene stack) written in HTML5 Canvas and
  vanilla JavaScript and shared with Donghyunhang. The land and sea tiles
  are derived from Natural Earth 110m land data (public domain); province
  adjacency and all numbers are test values chosen for this work and were
  not checked against the original game. No graphic, sound, text, number or
  code of the original game or of Sangokushi DS 2 is used or extracted. Romance of the Three
  Kingdoms is a series of Koei Tecmo Games; this work is unaffiliated. Input
  is keyboard, mouse or touch, with a fullscreen mode; the whole game can be
  played from the keyboard alone. A game can be saved by hand to three
  browser slots or to a .dhsave file and loaded back, together with the
  applied settings, the calendar and the random-number state. Sound effects
  are synthesized in the browser with the Web Audio API (no audio files)
  and can be switched off with a header button or the M key. A start card
  waits on the map until the player presses Sortie. The map shades the sea
  in three depths by distance from shore and tints land with the colour of
  the nearest ruler. The MIT
  license applies to the Donghyunsam code and documentation; the page fonts
  are under the SIL Open Font License. See LICENSE and CREDITS.md.
keywords:
  - turn-based strategy
  - strategy simulation game
  - Romance of the Three Kingdoms
  - hex grid
  - esoteric programming language
  - JavaScript
